HARDWARE SALES & MARKETING PLATFORM — PRODUCT BLUEPRINT

Version: 2.0.0
Status: Proposed specification
Project stage: Planning and technical design
Product type: Reusable, multi-business-capable platform

1. Product Vision

Build a secure, mobile-first platform that helps hardware businesses present products, receive enquiries, prepare quotations, manage sales leads and measure attributable sales.

The platform must be reusable. A business must be configurable without changing the core application code for every new client.

The first implementation will serve one authorized business. The architecture must prepare for future businesses while ensuring that each business's records remain isolated.

The initial release will not process live customer payments.

2. Product Principles

- Mobile usability and reliable operation take priority.
- Business data must be accurate and traceable.
- Prices and stock availability must never be invented.
- The platform must remain usable without AI services.
- Security and privacy must be designed before implementation.
- Financial records must be auditable.
- New features must not compromise existing functionality.
- Avoid unnecessary complexity and recurring costs.

3. Business Configuration and Branding

Each business must eventually have configurable:

- Business name and approved logo.
- Contact information and location.
- Brand colors and approved visual assets.
- Product categories and subcategories.
- Product catalogue and selling units.
- Enquiry and quotation contact routes.
- Applicable business terms and policies.

A business's branding and information must not be hardcoded into shared application logic.

No business identity, branding, product data or contact details may be used in a live deployment without authorization.

Mwangaza Hardware is a possible first client or demonstration business, subject to the owner's approval. It is not the permanent identity of the platform.

4. Main Customer Navigation

The initial website may include:

1. Home.
2. Products.
3. Materials Calculator.
4. Learning Centre.
5. Help and FAQs.
6. About the Business.
7. Contact.

A basket or selected-items summary must be accessible on mobile and desktop.

Navigation labels and sections should be configurable where practical.

5. Product Categories

The first hardware-business configuration may use these categories:

1. Building Items.
2. Plumbing Appliances and Accessories.
3. Electrical Items.
4. Electronics Items.
5. Water Supply Items.
6. Interior Design Items.
7. Home Appliances.

Categories and subcategories must be manageable without requiring code changes.

A product must only appear in categories to which it has intentionally been assigned.

6. Product Catalogue

A product record should support:

- Unique identifier.
- Business identifier.
- Product name.
- Main category and optional subcategory.
- Product images.
- Description.
- Brand, model and specifications where relevant.
- Selling unit.
- Confirmed selling price, when available.
- Availability status, when verified.
- Search keywords.
- Publication status.
- Created and updated timestamps.
- Record history where required.

Product management rules

Authorized administrators must eventually be able to:

- Add products.
- Edit product details.
- Change prices.
- Update availability.
- Publish or unpublish products.
- Archive discontinued products.
- Manage categories and product attributes.

Product attributes must be configurable where appropriate. Changes to an attribute must not silently erase historical quotation or sales information that depended on it.

Use archival or deactivation instead of destructive deletion when records are referenced by enquiries, quotations or sales.

Sample data must be visibly identified as demonstration data.

7. Product Browsing and Search

Customers should be able to:

- Browse categories.
- Search by product name, brand or keyword.
- Filter by relevant attributes.
- View product details and specifications.
- See confirmed prices and selling units.
- See whether a quotation is required.
- Add products to a selection basket.

Browsing and search must remain functional without AI services.

Search must handle empty results, spelling variations where practical, and unavailable products gracefully.

8. Basket and Quotation Requests

The first release provides a selection basket, not live checkout.

Customers should be able to:

- Add products.
- Enter quantities.
- Change quantities.
- Remove items.
- Continue browsing.
- Review selected items.
- View eligible line totals and estimated subtotal.
- Include products requiring a quotation.
- Submit a quotation request.

Calculation rules

Line amount = Quantity × Confirmed unit price.

Subtotal = Sum of eligible line amounts.

Do not calculate an unknown price as zero.

Do not present an estimated subtotal as a final payable amount.

Taxes, discounts, delivery charges and other fees must only be calculated using approved business rules.

The system must identify which items have confirmed prices and which require a quotation.

Basket persistence must consider shared-device privacy, browser storage limitations and accidental data loss.

9. Measurement and Units

The platform must support metric and imperial units where appropriate.

Examples include:

- Millimetres and inches.
- Metres and feet.
- Square metres and square feet.
- Kilograms and pounds.
- Litres and gallons.

Unit conversions must use documented conversion factors and consistent rounding rules.

The system must not add or compare incompatible units.

Original entered values and units should be retained where needed for quotation and audit purposes.

10. Enquiry and Quotation Workflow

The customer should provide only the information necessary to process an enquiry.

The system must:

- Validate required fields on the server.
- Protect submitted customer information.
- Record the selected products and quantities.
- Generate a unique enquiry reference.
- Confirm receipt only after successful submission.
- Prevent or identify accidental duplicate submissions.
- Provide a clear failure message and alternative contact route.
- Allow authorized staff to review and respond.
- Preserve enquiry history.

A controlled WhatsApp handoff may be offered as an alternative. It must not be represented as a successfully received enquiry unless receipt is verified.

A quotation becomes confirmed only after an authorized business representative verifies prices, availability and terms.

11. Materials Calculator

The calculator should use authoritative product and pricing information wherever applicable.

It must support suitable quantities, units, line amounts and totals.

Calculation formulas, conversion factors, rounding rules and assumptions must be documented and tested.

Unknown prices, taxes, delivery charges or discounts must not be invented.

Where calculations are estimates, the interface must clearly identify them as estimates.

12. Learning Centre and Customer Support

The Learning Centre may provide reviewed guidance on:

- Building-material selection.
- Plumbing products and fittings.
- Electrical-product basics and safety.
- Water storage and pumping.
- Interior finishes and lighting.
- Home-appliance selection.
- Material estimation and purchasing mistakes.

Safety-critical instructions must be reviewed and must not encourage unqualified work.

Customer support may include FAQs, verified contact details, approved WhatsApp links and other authorized channels.

AI or automated support must be assessed for accuracy, privacy, reliability and operating cost before activation.

13. Development Phases

Phase 1 — Website MVP

- Business-configured website.
- Product catalogue and search.
- Product details.
- Basket and quotation requests.
- Materials calculator.
- Learning content.
- Contact channels.
- Basic privacy and analytics controls.

No live payments, commission deductions or owner financial dashboard.

Phase 2 — Business Operations

- Secure administrator authentication.
- Product and category management.
- Enquiry management.
- Quotation workflow.
- Sales evidence records where authorized.
- Audit history.
- Role-based permissions.

Phase 3 — Owner Dashboard

- Private business reporting.
- Read-only access to business records.
- Enquiry and quotation summaries.
- Approved sales reports.
- Correction-request submission.
- Audit and correction history.

The owner dashboard must not allow owners to silently edit or delete financial, attribution or historical records.

Phase 4 — Commission and Growth

- Verified sales attribution.
- Commission calculation and reconciliation.
- Campaign performance reporting.
- Financial adjustments and dispute handling.
- Multi-business onboarding.
- Additional integrations after security and cost review.

Each phase must pass its own acceptance criteria before being treated as complete.

14. Sales Attribution and Commission

Commission is a future feature and requires a written agreement with the participating business.

The agreement and implementation must define:

- What qualifies as a completed sale.
- Which evidence proves the sale.
- Attribution rules for website, walk-in, phone and WhatsApp customers.
- Attribution window.
- Repeat purchases.
- Partial payments.
- Refunds and cancellations.
- Commission rate and calculation basis.
- Commission due date.
- Disputes and adjustments.

An enquiry, quotation request or click alone is not a completed sale.

Commission must not be calculated as payable until the relevant sale and agreement conditions are verified.

Advertising spend, business revenue, provider fees and agency commission must be accounted for separately.

Customer payments must go to an authorized business merchant account, never an agency personal account.

15. Owner Dashboard and Correction Requests

The owner dashboard is private and read-only for business records.

Owners may view authorized reports and submit correction requests.

Owners must not directly edit or delete:

- Sales records.
- Payment evidence.
- Commission rates or accrued commission.
- Attribution rules.
- Audit history.
- User permissions.
- Developer or system controls.

A correction request must contain:

- Affected record.
- Description of the issue.
- Proposed correction.
- Reason.
- Supporting evidence where available.

Request states:

1. Submitted.
2. Under review.
3. Approved.
4. Rejected.
5. More information required.
6. Resolved.

Every decision must record the responsible administrator, date and time, reason and resulting action.

Original values and financial history must be preserved. Approved corrections must create traceable adjustments rather than silently overwrite accrued or paid records.

These restrictions must be enforced by database permissions and server-side authorization, not merely by hiding interface controls.

16. Developer and Administrator Workspace

Authorized administrators may eventually manage:

- Approved business configuration.
- Products and categories.
- Enquiries and quotations.
- Sales evidence.
- Commission reconciliation.
- Correction requests.
- User access and permissions.
- Campaign and reporting configuration.
- Operational monitoring.

Privileged actions must be authenticated, authorized, validated and audited.

No administrator should have unnecessary access to customer information or financial records.

17. Multi-Business Data Isolation

The initial release may serve one business, but the data model must be designed for future business separation.

Business-owned records must be associated with a business identifier.

Database access policies must ensure that one business cannot read or change another business's records.

Tenant isolation must be tested at the database and server layers.

Do not rely on interface filtering as a security boundary.

Multi-business onboarding must not be activated until authorization, permissions, isolation and recovery procedures have been verified.

18. Security, Privacy and Reliability

Required controls include:

- Secure authentication.
- Server-side authorization.
- Database-level access policies.
- Input validation and output encoding.
- Protection against spam and abusive requests.
- Rate limiting where appropriate.
- Secure secret management.
- HTTPS in production.
- Data minimization and clear privacy notices.
- Defined data retention and deletion procedures.
- Protected file uploads and storage.
- Audit logging for sensitive actions.
- Backups for database and stored files.
- Tested restoration procedures.
- Monitoring and error reporting.
- Documented incident and recovery procedures.

No system can be guaranteed never to fail. The objective is to reduce foreseeable failures, detect incidents and recover without unacceptable data loss.

19. Performance, Accessibility and Device Compatibility

The platform must:

- Use responsive mobile-first layouts.
- Work on common Android devices and browsers.
- Optimize images and other assets.
- Avoid unnecessary scripts and dependencies.
- Provide loading, empty, error and success states.
- Use accessible labels and keyboard navigation.
- Maintain readable contrast and text sizes.
- Test slow connections and interrupted submissions.
- Monitor performance after deployment.

20. Initial Website Acceptance Criteria

Phase 1 is not ready for release until:

- Approved business configuration is present.
- Product categories and catalogue operate correctly.
- Search and filters work.
- Product details display accurate information.
- Basket operations work.
- Calculations use verified prices and compatible units.
- Unpriced products can be included in enquiries.
- Enquiry submission and failure states are tested.
- Customer information is protected.
- The site works on representative Android devices.
- Required automated tests pass.
- Security checks pass.
- Backup and recovery arrangements are documented.
- Release and rollback procedures are tested.

21. Implementation Principle

Build incrementally. Define the architecture before coding, test each change, preserve working functionality and document important decisions.

Do not introduce future-phase features into the MVP without an approved reason.

Do not claim that a feature, security control or release is complete without verification evidence.

The objective is a maintainable, secure and commercially usable platform that can grow without sacrificing data integrity or reliability.
